id: design.connection-pool
kind: design
title: Connection pooling
aliases: connection pool, pool sizing, connection management
tags: database, performance, reliability
questions:
  - how many database connections should a service use?
invariant: A connection pool is a concurrency limit on a shared resource; its size is bounded by the resource, not by the client's parallelism.
answer_30s: Pool at one shared instance per process, size it from the database's capacity divided by replicas, set acquire and idle timeouts, and treat waiting for a connection as backpressure.
answer_2m: |
  The arithmetic that interviewers want: connections are not free on the
  database side (memory, scheduler slots, lock contention), so
  total_connections = replicas * pool_size must stay below the database's limit,
  and a smaller pool with queueing in the application is usually faster than a
  large pool with thrashing. Timeouts are the safety net: an acquire timeout
  turns "everything is slow" into "some requests fail fast", and idle timeouts
  release connections after a burst. Pool saturation is a metric you should
  alert on, because it is the earliest visible sign that a dependency is
  degrading, before latency and error rate move. For services calling other
  services the same reasoning applies to the HTTP client's connection pool and
  keep-alive settings.
when_to_use:
  - every database or broker client; pooled HTTP clients for outbound calls
failure_modes:
  - pool per request, so pooling is defeated and connections churn
  - replicas * pool_size exceeding the database's max_connections
  - no acquire timeout, so a slow database becomes unbounded request latency
  - long-running transactions holding connections while the pool empties
related:
  - rust.connection-pool
  - design.backpressure
  - design.timeouts
  - rust.app-state
  - recipe.database-backed-api
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/sqlx/latest/sqlx/struct.Pool.html
  - https://wiki.postgresql.org/wiki/Number_Of_Database_Connections
